<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Metapontum</h1><p class="st-meta">630 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.) · capital Metapontum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Achaean Greek city on the Gulf of Taranto, rich in grain, where Pythagoras spent his last years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Achaean settlers founded Metapontum in the 7th century BCE on the coastal plain between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bradano">Bradano</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basento">Basento</a> rivers. Ancient sources say <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sybaris">Sybaris</a> encouraged the colony to block the expansion of Spartan <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a>. Earlier native and Greek settlement existed nearby, and the city grew quickly on the plain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Metapontum's wealth came from grain, celebrated on its coins with an ear of barley and by a 'golden harvest' it dedicated at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>. Archaeologists have traced the division of its countryside into regular plots and many farmhouses. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tavole-palatine">Tavole Palatine</a>, fifteen standing columns of a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hera">Hera</a>, survive outside the town. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pythagoras">Pythagoras</a> moved there after being driven from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/croton">Croton</a>, and his school stayed influential in the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Metapontum joined the Athenian side in 413 BCE and later suffered from Lucanian pressure. It came under Roman control after the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> around 272 BCE, sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> and was abandoned by many of its people when he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 207 BCE. It survived as a small Roman town.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/metapontum.jpg" alt="Metapontum" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: AlexanderVanLoon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metapontum_theater_AvL.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>630 BCE – 272 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Metapontum</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Achaean Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Metapontion</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-451&amp;focus=metapontum" aria-label="Metapontum on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5093 733 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Metapontum lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5413 989L5300 825M5413 989L5304 942M5413 989L5216 1012"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5300 825h0M5304 942h0M5216 1012h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5413 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5413 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-451&amp;focus=metapontum">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

